### 2. Are property taxes in the United States levied at the federal, state, or local level, and does the federal government have the authority to impose a nationwide property tax?
Answer: Property taxes in the United States are primarily levied at the **local** level (by cities, counties, school districts, and other local jurisdictions), though some states also levy taxes on personal property. According to the Urban Institute, "the tax on real property is primarily levied by local governments (cities, counties, and school districts) rather than state governments," with local governments collecting $581 billion in property taxes in 2020 (30% of local general revenue) compared to only $19 billion at the state level (1% of state general revenue). There is **no federal property tax** in the United States; the federal government does not levy or collect property taxes, and the search result makes no mention of any such federal authority or practice. This makes the concept of a "US wide 2% property tax increase" proposed by the president fundamentally implausible, as property taxation falls outside the jurisdiction of the federal government.

Source URL: https://www.urban.org/policy-centers/cross-center-initiatives/state-and-local-finance-initiative/projects/state-and-local-backgrounders/property-taxes

